## Why This Is Asked

Standard quicksort performs well on average but can degrade to O(n²) on adversarial inputs, particularly with many duplicates. Introsort-style hybrids combine the practical efficiency of quicksort with worst-case guarantees, making-world engineering this a common real constraint.

## Key Concepts

- **3-way partitioning (Dutch National Flag)**: Partitions array into elements less than, equal to, and greater than the pivot, dramatically reducing comparisons for datasets with many duplicates
- **Recursion depth tracking**: Prevents stack overflow and performance degradation on pathological inputs
- **Hybrid switching**: Falls back to mergesort or heapsort when depth exceeds 2*log2(N) or partition size drops below a threshold (~16 elements)
- **Worst-case guarantees**: Ensures O(n log n) time complexity regardless of input distribution

## Code Example

```javascript
function hybridSort(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const maxDepth = 2 * Math.floor(Math.log2(n));

  function quickSort3Way(lo, hi, depth) {
    if (hi - lo < 16) {
      insertionSort(arr, lo, hi);
      return;
    }
    if (depth > maxDepth) {
      mergeSort(arr, lo, hi);
      return;
    }

    const [lt, gt] = partition3Way(arr, lo, hi);
    quickSort3Way(lo, lt - 1, depth + 1);
    quickSort3Way(gt + 1, hi, depth + 1);
  }

  quickSort3Way(0, n - 1, 0);
  return arr;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes understanding of sorting trade-offs (in-place memory vs stability vs constants) and how hybrid approaches balance speed, especially relevant in finance and large-scale data.

## Key Concepts

- 3-way partitioning in quicksort
- Insertion sort for small slices
- In-place sorting and O(1) extra space
- Average vs worst-case complexities; pivot choice

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortHybrid(a){
  const TH = 16;
  const swap=(i,j)=>{ [a[i],a[j]]=[a[j],a[i]]; };
  const insertSort=(l,r)=>{
    for(let i=l+1;i<=r;i++){
      let x=a[i], j=i-1;
      while(j>=l && a[j]>x){ a[j+1]=a[j]; j--; }
      a[j+1]=x;
    }
  };
  const partition=(lo,hi)=>{
    const p=a[lo+((hi-lo)>>1)];
    let i=lo, lt=lo, gt=hi;
    while(i<=gt){
      if(a[i]<p){ swap(i,lt); i++; lt++; }
      else if(a[i]>p){ swap(i,gt); gt--; }
      else i++;
    }
    return [lt,gt];
  };
  const qs=(lo,hi)=>{
    if(lo>=hi) return;
    if(hi-lo+1 <= TH){ insertSort(lo,hi); return; }
    const [l,r] = partition(lo,hi);
    qs(lo, l-1);
    qs(r+1, hi);
  };
  qs(0,a.length-1);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to implement a Trie with efficient prefix queries, which is fundamental for autocomplete features. The per-node counter design enables O(k) prefix counting where k is the prefix length, and O(m) insertion where m is the word length.

## Key Concepts

- Trie structure with per-node subtree counts
- Incrementing counts during insertion for O(1) prefix queries
- Prefix traversal and counting
- Edge case handling for empty strings, null inputs, and non-existent prefixes

## Code Example

```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.subtreeCount = 0;
    this.isWord = false;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  insert(word) {
    if (!word || typeof word !== 'string') {
      return;
    }

    let node = this.root;
    node.subtreeCount++;

    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
      node.subtreeCount++;
    }

    node.isWord = true;
  }

  getPrefixCount(prefix) {
    if (!prefix || typeof prefix !== 'string') {
      return 0;
    }

    let node = this.root;

    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        return 0;
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }

    return node.subtreeCount;
  }
}

// Example usage:
const trie = new Trie();
trie.insert('cat');
trie.insert('cater');
trie.insert('category');
trie.insert('dog');

console.log(trie.getPrefixCount('cat'));  // 3 (cat, cater, category)
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount('ca'));   // 3
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount('c'));    // 3
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount('dog'));  // 1
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount('z'));    // 0 (non-existent prefix)
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount(''));     // 0 (empty string)
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount(null));   // 0 (null input)
```

## Complexity Analysis

- **Time Complexity**: O(m) for insert, O(k) for getPrefixCount where m is word length and k is prefix length
- **Space Complexity**: O(ALPHABET_SIZE × m × n) where m is average word length and n is number of words, but optimized with Map-based children

